{
  "id": "system_configuration",
  "version": "1.5.0",
  "host_api": 6,
  "capabilities": [
    "Configure application dependencies, reviewed NixOS enable options and caller-bound groups"
  ],
  "permissions": [
    "packages"
  ],
  "instructions": "Compose the supporting packages, reviewed NixOS enable options, caller groups and typed PostgreSQL setup needed by the selected application using install_package.setup. Preserve explicit tools-only intent. For ambiguous server software installs ask whether the user wants a local server or command-line tools; for explicit service requests include the required setup in the same proposal. PostgreSQL setup uses a versioned package and optional caller_database for a local peer-authenticated database owned by the requesting user. Existing administrator-managed servers, remote access, arbitrary database credentials and major-version migrations require separate administration. Explain unsupported requirements; never silently fall back to installing executables. No application-name routing recipes. Only the host setting catalogue is available; unsupported settings require a host update.\n\nWhen any requirement is outside the catalogue, give concrete, numbered manual steps in message: state what Peasy can apply and what remains, describe where to change the application or NixOS configuration, show the needed settings/commands when known, and mention any logout/reboot, credentials entered locally, or administrator work. For declarative changes explain how to edit a module imported by the current host, retaining destination hardware configuration, and rebuild using that host's existing traditional or flake workflow; do not invent a flake path or output name. Never edit Peasy's generated managed module manually. Ask a focused question if missing information prevents accurate instructions. Do not invent configuration keys, hardware drivers, package names or claim that suggestions were tested. Manual commands/snippets in explanation text are guidance only, never executable Peasy actions. If partial installation would be useful and consistent with the request, include supported setup in install_package and put the remaining steps in message. If required details are missing, the user requested a fully working unsupported service, or partial setup would mislead them, return explain with the steps and make no changes. Flatpak integration does not install remotes/apps or grant sandbox overrides; describe those remaining steps. Unfree software policy, vendor/32-bit GPU drivers, audio-stack replacement, arbitrary udev rules, app configuration, remote listeners/firewall ports, secrets and migrations still need manual steps when relevant. Do not launch applications or run extra readiness probes after installation. A successful build/activation means the reviewed configuration was applied, not that every application workflow was verified. Request device groups only for the specific access the user needs; active desktop seats often already have access. Prefer rootless Podman when it meets the request; never silently replace an explicit Docker request, and clearly explain that docker group membership is effectively root access.",
